# Settings for docker compose. Copy this file to .env and edit the copy:
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# .env is ignored by git and by the image build. Never commit it, and never put a key into this
# example file. A line that starts with # is off. A value with spaces, a $ or a # in it needs
# single quotes; the generated values suggested below need none.
#
# Running from source (pnpm dev) does not read this file. Set the same names in your shell.
# Do not paste the output of `docker compose config` anywhere: it prints the values set here.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. Required: the database password
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The password of the PostgreSQL that compose starts. Compose refuses to start while it is empty.
# Make one with:  openssl rand -hex 24
# It is set when the database volume is first created. Changing it here later does not change it
# inside the database: the app then cannot sign in, until you either change the password in
# PostgreSQL too or delete the volume (which deletes every review).
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. The model: choose one of the three ways, and leave the other two off
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The text of every contract you review is sent to the model you choose here, and nowhere else.
# The web app shows where, on its first screen. Without a working choice, the app starts and says
# so, and no review can run.

# --- A. Anthropic (the default provider) ------------------------------------------------------
# Contract text goes to the Anthropic API (api.anthropic.com).
# CLAUSEWISE_PROVIDER=anthropic
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
# CLAUSEWISE_MODEL=              leave unset to use the default model
# CLAUSEWISE_EFFORT=             low, medium, high, xhigh or max; unset uses the model's default
# CLAUSEWISE_FALLBACK=           "default" lets the Anthropic API rerun a declined request on a
#                                second model. Leave unset if the text may go to only one.

# --- B. OpenAI --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Contract text goes to the OpenAI API (api.openai.com).
# CLAUSEWISE_PROVIDER=openai
# OPENAI_API_KEY=
# CLAUSEWISE_MODEL=              required: the exact model name your account offers

# --- C. An OpenAI-compatible server, such as Ollama ---------------------------------------------
# Contract text goes to the server at CLAUSEWISE_BASE_URL. With the ollama profile of this
# compose file that is a container on your own machine, and nothing leaves it.
#
# To use the Ollama that compose can start:
#   1. Turn the profile on:  COMPOSE_PROFILES=ollama   (or start with: docker compose --profile ollama up -d)
#   2. Download a model once:  docker compose exec ollama ollama pull <model>
#   3. Set the four lines below, with the model name exactly as you pulled it.
#
# COMPOSE_PROFILES=ollama
# CLAUSEWISE_PROVIDER=openai-compatible
# CLAUSEWISE_BASE_URL=http://ollama:11434/v1
# CLAUSEWISE_MODEL=
#
# A local model has a small context window and needs a limit here, or a long contract is sent
# in one piece and fails. Set CLAUSEWISE_MAX_CONTEXT_TOKENS below the window the model really
# has; Clausewise then reviews a longer contract in parts. It must leave room for about 4,800
# tokens of instructions and answer in every call, so a limit below about 5,900 is refused with
# the starter playbooks, and a larger playbook needs more.
# Ollama's own window is set by OLLAMA_CONTEXT_LENGTH, which compose passes to the ollama
# service (16384 if unset here). The two numbers below are examples, not tested recommendations.
#
# CLAUSEWISE_MAX_CONTEXT_TOKENS=14000
# OLLAMA_CONTEXT_LENGTH=16384
#
# Another server (a vLLM or llama.cpp server, a gateway) works the same way: leave the profile
# off, set CLAUSEWISE_BASE_URL to its API root, and set CLAUSEWISE_API_KEY if it wants a key.
# From the container, your own machine is host.docker.internal on Docker Desktop. On Linux, add
# extra_hosts: ["host.docker.internal:host-gateway"] to the app service in an override file.
# CLAUSEWISE_API_KEY=

# --- Tuning, for any provider ------------------------------------------------------------------
# CLAUSEWISE_CONCURRENCY=4            rule checks in flight at once for one review
# CLAUSEWISE_MAX_OUTPUT_TOKENS=16000  most tokens in one model answer
# CLAUSEWISE_MAX_RETRIES=2            retries after a network error or an unusable answer
# CLAUSEWISE_MAX_CONTEXT_TOKENS=      see section C; for a hosted model, unset sends the whole contract

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 3. The server
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The port on this computer. The web app is published on 127.0.0.1 only, so other computers
# cannot reach it. To let them, put a reverse proxy in front (see the README), do not change this.
# CLAUSEWISE_PORT=8787

# A shared access token. Every request to the API must carry it, and the web app asks for it
# once. At least 16 characters. Make one with:  openssl rand -hex 24
# Set one whenever anyone but you can reach the port, and always behind a reverse proxy.
# CLAUSEWISE_ACCESS_TOKEN=

# Names other than localhost that people use to reach the server, separated by commas, for a
# reverse proxy. Without an access token, a request addressed to any other name is refused, which
# is what stops a web page from reaching the server through DNS rebinding. The proxy must pass the
# original Host header on.
# CLAUSEWISE_ALLOWED_HOSTS=contracts.example.com

# Largest upload, in megabytes (1 to 1024).
# CLAUSEWISE_MAX_UPLOAD_MB=25

# Reviews that run at once (1 to 16), and how many may wait. Others get "too many requests".
# CLAUSEWISE_REVIEW_CONCURRENCY=1
# CLAUSEWISE_MAX_QUEUED=20
